Security Reflection
Connecting an application backend directly as the root user poses significant security risks. The root account possesses unrestricted administrative privileges (DROP databases, modify system settings, access all data across all databases). If the application suffers an SQL Injection vulnerability or if backend credentials leak, an attacker gains complete control over the entire database server.
Least-Privileged Application User Configuration:
-- Create a dedicated app user
CREATE USER 'school_app_user'@'localhost' IDENTIFIED BY 'StrongPassword123!';

-- Grant only necessary permissions on the school database
GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON school.* TO 'school_app_user'@'localhost';

-- Apply changes
FLUSH PRIVILEGES;

